<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xml:lang="EN" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Neural Transplant Plast</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">NP</journal-id><journal-title>Journal of Neural Transplantation &#x00026; Plasticity</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0792-8483</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2565169</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1155/NP.1992.240</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Defining the Range of Cellular Components, Including Internal Antigens, That Can Serve as Targets of Graft Rejection</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Lampson</surname><given-names>L. A.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"/></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Lampson</surname><given-names>M. A.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"/></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Dunne</surname><given-names>A. D.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"/></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="I1">Center for Neurologic Diseases, Department of Neurology, Brigham and Women's Hospital and Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA 02115, USA</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>1992</year></pub-date><volume>3</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>240</fpage><lpage>241</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1992.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1992</copyright-year></permissions><abstract><p>The mechanisms underlying rejection of grafted neural tissue are still being defined. Mechanisms relevant to genetically engineered cells are of current interest. To date, attention has focused on major histocompatibility complex (MHC) antigens as targets of graft rejection. Yet even when there is no MHC disparity, as when the patient's own cells are genetically altered, there is still a potential for graft rejection, directed against the novel antigens. We illustrate this in a rat model.</p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>